+  // re4-port: DVDReadAsyncPrio's own bounds check (above, `offset + length < fileInfo->length +
+  // DVD_MIN_TRANSFER_SIZE`) explicitly documents that a request may extend up to
+  // DVD_MIN_TRANSFER_SIZE (32) bytes past a file's logical end -- real GameCube DVD reads are
+  // always whole 32-byte-aligned sector transfers, so a caller that rounds its request size up to
+  // the next 32-byte boundary (src/game/dvd.cpp's cDvdQueue::fileReadAsync: `ALIGN32(size)`) gets
+  // those trailing alignment bytes for free from whatever real data follows on the disc, never a
+  // short/failed read. `handle->read()` above stops at the *file's own* logical EOF (its FST-
+  // registered length) rather than the physical disc image, so this padding request would
+  // otherwise come back short and get reported as a real transfer-size mismatch by the caller's own
+  // callback (result != length) -- a false DVD read error the real hardware would never raise.
+  // Zero-fill the (small, spec-bounded) shortfall instead of truncating the reported count, exactly
+  // the same way the real disc's physical padding bytes are never inspected by the caller anyway
+  // (dvd.cpp only copies out the file's own true, unpadded length once the transfer completes).
+  // A shortfall bigger than DVD_MIN_TRANSFER_SIZE is a real short read (wrong offset/length, or a
+  // genuinely truncated disc image), not alignment padding -- left as a real (correctly reported)
+  // short/failed transfer.
+  if (remaining > 0 && remaining <= DVD_MIN_TRANSFER_SIZE) {
+    std::memset(writePtr + totalRead, 0, static_cast<size_t>(remaining));
+    totalRead += remaining;
+    remaining = 0;
+  }
 
